Jim Miller <yohocoma@yahoo.com> wrote:
> On Mon, 04 Dec 2006 14:05:12 +0100, Patric Mueller <bhaak@bigfoot.com>
> wrote:
>
>> Well, nobody keeps those talented programmers to release their own
>> versions of NetHack. This is how SlashEm started.
>
>
> Why ever coordinate on any software project - or on anything - then?
> Why try to combine the talents of interested people?   Why not always
> have multiple groups or islands of people working in parallel,
> duplicating effort? 

I don't see a lot of duplicated effort in the development of Nethack
patches. Where do you see this effort?

> Hell, why bother writing software libraries?

Writing software libraries and writing games are two completely
different things and their development is different.

The goal of software libraries is way more technical and to find a
consensus what to do is much easier than with games. It is not
surprising that there are much more good open source software
libraries out there than good open source games. 

> There's a couple nethack spinoff projects that have gained some good
> traction, but I think most of the work out there is born out of lack
> of ability to truly contribute, rather than a desire to strike out on
> one's own.

Why do you think that those contributions would have been incorporated
into the official version when the DevTeam would act like a normal
open source development team? Even Linus doesn't accept everything
that the developer wants to be in the linux kernel.

When the goals of the developer are different they have to be on
different projects.

Compare again NetHack and SlashEm. Both games try to achieve
completely different things. You wouldn't be able to combine that into
one project.

> And frequently, an idea for a feature will be discussed,
> but no one wants to do it because of the hugeness of the project -
> think portable bones or OO nethack.  With central, coordinated
> development and division of labor, these things could have happened
> years ago.

I doubt that. When no one wants to do it, why should that be easier
with central, coordinated (who would coordinate) development?
Especially with a huge undertaking like OO-nethack. You can't do that
in small incremental steps.

> The current devtem paradigm stands in the way.  It matters
> that people believe their work is "official".

So what? SlashEm is not the official "NetHack", NetHack is not the
official "Hack", Hack wasn't the official "Rogue".

